From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:04:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Return VideoToolbox packets on the call that sends their
 frame

<details><summary>Claude's draft</summary>

Performance improvements for the VideoToolbox encoders, h264 and hevc.

Both now set flags=+low_delay, which turns on VideoToolbox's low-latency
rate control, and request NV12, the layout VideoToolbox encodes from.
Before this, each packet came back from the encode() call after the one
that sent its frame, one frame (33 ms at 30 fps) late.

With yuv420p input, low_delay is on time only with an FFmpeg whose
videotoolboxenc.c waits for the encoded frame in low_delay mode (stock
9.0.2 does not). NV12 with low_delay is on time on stock FFmpeg too.

hevc drops constant_bit_rate=1: low-latency mode is a rate control of its
own, and hevc_videotoolbox fails to open with both set.

Verified on Apple Silicon, 1280x720, one frame every 33 ms, 60 frames:
- On an FFmpeg 9.0.2 with that patch, both encoders returned a packet on
  every call; before, the first call returned none and each later packet
  came one call late.
- On stock conda-forge FFmpeg 9.0.2, tests/test_h264.py went from 9
  failures to 2. The 7 now passing expect a packet for every frame. The 2
  remaining also failed before: the default target bitrate, and the
  fragment count in test_encoder_large.
- ruff reports the same 11 errors in these files as before.

